1
00:00:00,182 --> 00:00:03,090
Square roots, fractions
with x in the bottom,

2
00:00:03,110 --> 00:00:06,734
absolute value bars, and a
line crossing a parabola.

3
00:00:06,754 --> 00:00:09,519
These are nonlinear equations and systems,

4
00:00:09,539 --> 00:00:13,003
one of the three Advanced
Math skills on the SAT.

5
00:00:13,055 --> 00:00:15,142
Most students can do the algebra.

6
00:00:15,162 --> 00:00:18,714
They lose the point at the end, by
keeping a solution that doesn't work,

7
00:00:18,734 --> 00:00:20,555
or losing one that does.

8
00:00:20,632 --> 00:00:24,826
In this video: radical,
rational, absolute value,

9
00:00:24,846 --> 00:00:28,505
and exponent equations,
lines that meet parabolas,

10
00:00:28,525 --> 00:00:30,005
and Desmos checks.

11
00:00:30,025 --> 00:00:33,096
Seven practice questions, easy to hard.

12
00:00:33,140 --> 00:00:35,461
Here's the idea behind this whole topic.

13
00:00:35,747 --> 00:00:39,370
Some algebra moves can add fake solutions.

14
00:00:39,390 --> 00:00:41,319
Others can lose real ones.

15
00:00:41,355 --> 00:00:44,426
Know which is which, and
you'll know when to check.

16
00:00:44,472 --> 00:00:45,381
Squaring adds.

17
00:00:45,401 --> 00:00:47,238
Start with x equals three.

18
00:00:47,258 --> 00:00:48,238
One solution.

19
00:00:48,258 --> 00:00:51,488
Square both sides, and you
get x squared equals nine,

20
00:00:51,508 --> 00:00:54,381
which has two solutions,
three and negative three.

21
00:00:54,401 --> 00:00:57,059
Negative three never solved
the original equation.

22
00:00:57,079 --> 00:00:58,606
Squaring created it.

23
00:00:58,626 --> 00:01:02,285
Multiplying both sides by an
expression with x can add one too,

24
00:01:02,305 --> 00:01:05,356
often a value that makes
a denominator zero.

25
00:01:05,376 --> 00:01:07,963
These fakes are called
extraneous solutions.

26
00:01:07,983 --> 00:01:10,606
So after you square or clear fractions,

27
00:01:10,626 --> 00:01:12,198
plug your answers back in.

28
00:01:12,248 --> 00:01:13,677
Dividing loses.

29
00:01:14,213 --> 00:01:16,391
Take x squared equals five x.

30
00:01:16,570 --> 00:01:20,105
Divide both sides by x,
and you get x equals five.

31
00:01:20,248 --> 00:01:22,034
But zero works too.

32
00:01:22,177 --> 00:01:26,550
Zero squared is zero, and
five times zero is zero.

33
00:01:26,570 --> 00:01:28,677
Dividing by x threw it away.

34
00:01:28,707 --> 00:01:31,222
The fix is to factor instead.

35
00:01:31,242 --> 00:01:34,580
x times the quantity x
minus five equals zero,

36
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:36,779
so x is zero or five.

37
00:01:36,856 --> 00:01:38,622
Absolute value can lose one too.

38
00:01:38,642 --> 00:01:43,535
The absolute value of x equals four
means x is four or negative four.

39
00:01:43,713 --> 00:01:46,176
Forget the negative
case, and you lose one.

40
00:01:46,229 --> 00:01:49,566
The College Board describes
this skill as solving quadratic,

41
00:01:49,586 --> 00:01:53,851
absolute value, rational,
radical, and polynomial equations,

42
00:01:53,871 --> 00:01:55,621
and systems of equations.

43
00:01:55,694 --> 00:01:58,316
It can also ask how many
real solutions there are,

44
00:01:58,336 --> 00:02:01,258
or which graph matches
a system's solutions.

45
00:02:01,278 --> 00:02:03,685
To spot the type, look at
what's wrapped around x.

46
00:02:03,705 --> 00:02:05,563
A square root.

47
00:02:05,778 --> 00:02:07,472
An x in a denominator.

48
00:02:07,492 --> 00:02:08,828
Absolute value bars.

49
00:02:08,848 --> 00:02:10,436
An x in an exponent.

50
00:02:10,456 --> 00:02:12,972
Or a second equation with a square.

51
00:02:12,992 --> 00:02:16,023
Each wrapper has its own
fix and its own trap.

52
00:02:16,043 --> 00:02:17,222
Question one.

53
00:02:18,043 --> 00:02:19,523
The square root of the
quantity x plus seven,

54
00:02:19,543 --> 00:02:21,722
plus five, equals x.

55
00:02:21,865 --> 00:02:24,433
What are all solutions
to the given equation?

56
00:02:24,453 --> 00:02:25,632
Pause and try it.

57
00:02:29,807 --> 00:02:31,786
First, isolate the square root.

58
00:02:31,806 --> 00:02:38,073
Subtract five: the square root of the
quantity x plus seven equals x minus five.

59
00:02:38,093 --> 00:02:40,572
Don't square while the
five is still attached,

60
00:02:40,592 --> 00:02:42,526
or the square root won't go away.

61
00:02:42,546 --> 00:02:47,767
Now square both sides: x plus seven
equals x squared minus ten x plus

62
00:02:47,787 --> 00:02:48,410
twenty-five.

63
00:02:48,430 --> 00:02:53,767
Rearrange: x squared minus eleven
x plus eighteen equals zero,

64
00:02:53,787 --> 00:02:57,982
which factors into x minus
two, times x minus nine.

65
00:02:58,002 --> 00:03:00,245
So x is two or nine.

66
00:03:00,265 --> 00:03:01,888
We squared, so check both.

67
00:03:01,908 --> 00:03:04,531
For nine, the square
root of sixteen is four,

68
00:03:04,551 --> 00:03:06,317
plus five is nine.

69
00:03:06,337 --> 00:03:07,281
That works.

70
00:03:07,301 --> 00:03:09,744
For two, the square root of nine is three,

71
00:03:09,764 --> 00:03:12,137
plus five is eight, not two.

72
00:03:12,157 --> 00:03:13,388
Two is extraneous.

73
00:03:13,408 --> 00:03:14,689
The answer is B.

74
00:03:14,709 --> 00:03:18,403
C is the trap: right algebra, no check.

75
00:03:18,423 --> 00:03:21,725
Faster tell: a square
root can't be negative,

76
00:03:21,745 --> 00:03:24,439
so x minus five can't be negative either.

77
00:03:24,459 --> 00:03:27,780
x has to be at least five,
so two was never possible.

78
00:03:27,836 --> 00:03:30,959
In Desmos, graph each
side as its own equation.

79
00:03:30,979 --> 00:03:34,493
The two graphs cross once, at nine, nine.

80
00:03:34,513 --> 00:03:35,655
Question two.

81
00:03:35,691 --> 00:03:41,350
x over the quantity x minus two, equals
eight over the quantity x squared minus

82
00:03:41,370 --> 00:03:42,171
four.

83
00:03:42,191 --> 00:03:44,797
What is the solution
to the given equation?

84
00:03:44,819 --> 00:03:45,997
Pause and try it.

85
00:03:50,172 --> 00:03:52,993
First, write down the excluded values.

86
00:03:53,030 --> 00:03:58,224
A denominator can't be zero, and x
squared minus four is x minus two,

87
00:03:58,244 --> 00:03:59,867
times x plus two.

88
00:03:59,887 --> 00:04:02,600
So x can't be two or negative two.

89
00:04:02,622 --> 00:04:04,194
Now clear the fractions.

90
00:04:04,337 --> 00:04:09,265
Multiply both sides by x
minus two, times x plus two.

91
00:04:09,408 --> 00:04:14,301
You get x times the quantity
x plus two equals eight,

92
00:04:14,514 --> 00:04:18,487
or x squared plus two x
minus eight equals zero.

93
00:04:18,507 --> 00:04:22,308
That factors into x plus
four, times x minus two,

94
00:04:22,328 --> 00:04:25,273
so x is negative four or two.

95
00:04:25,293 --> 00:04:26,701
But two is excluded.

96
00:04:26,721 --> 00:04:28,450
It makes the denominators zero.

97
00:04:28,470 --> 00:04:30,828
The only solution is negative four.

98
00:04:30,860 --> 00:04:31,447
Check it.

99
00:04:31,467 --> 00:04:34,303
Negative four over
negative six is two thirds,

100
00:04:34,323 --> 00:04:36,518
and eight over twelve is two thirds.

101
00:04:36,538 --> 00:04:39,287
If you typed two, you kept the fake one.

102
00:04:39,316 --> 00:04:40,511
Question three.

103
00:04:40,531 --> 00:04:44,404
Two times the absolute value
of the quantity x minus three,

104
00:04:44,424 --> 00:04:46,459
plus one, equals nine.

105
00:04:46,709 --> 00:04:49,530
What are all solutions
to the given equation?

106
00:04:49,558 --> 00:04:50,736
Pause and try it.

107
00:04:54,911 --> 00:04:57,876
Isolate the absolute value first.

108
00:04:58,232 --> 00:05:01,340
Subtract one, then divide by two.

109
00:05:01,447 --> 00:05:04,570
Dividing by two is safe,
because two is a number,

110
00:05:04,590 --> 00:05:06,697
not an expression with x.

111
00:05:07,197 --> 00:05:11,019
Now the absolute value of
x minus three equals four.

112
00:05:11,066 --> 00:05:12,974
Now split into two cases.

113
00:05:12,994 --> 00:05:15,832
The inside is either
four or negative four.

114
00:05:15,852 --> 00:05:18,780
x minus three equals four gives seven.

115
00:05:18,994 --> 00:05:22,474
x minus three equals negative
four gives negative one.

116
00:05:22,494 --> 00:05:24,093
The answer is B.

117
00:05:24,113 --> 00:05:27,521
A is the classic miss:
only the positive case.

118
00:05:27,541 --> 00:05:30,736
C pushes the two and
the one inside the bars.

119
00:05:30,756 --> 00:05:32,308
D flips the signs.

120
00:05:32,328 --> 00:05:35,164
Think distance: x is four away from three,

121
00:05:35,184 --> 00:05:37,033
so seven or negative one.

122
00:05:37,053 --> 00:05:38,176
One more trap.

123
00:05:38,196 --> 00:05:42,248
If isolating gives an absolute
value equal to a negative number,

124
00:05:42,268 --> 00:05:43,661
there's no solution.

125
00:05:43,732 --> 00:05:48,711
In Desmos, the left side is a V, and
the line y equals nine crosses it twice,

126
00:05:48,731 --> 00:05:50,581
at negative one and seven.

127
00:05:50,601 --> 00:05:51,815
Question four.

128
00:05:51,958 --> 00:05:58,100
Four to the power x plus one, equals
eight to the power x minus one.

129
00:05:58,137 --> 00:06:01,521
What value of x satisfies
the given equation?

130
00:06:01,541 --> 00:06:02,719
Pause and try it.

131
00:06:06,895 --> 00:06:12,053
When x is in the exponent, you can't
compare exponents until the bases match.

132
00:06:12,073 --> 00:06:14,803
Four and eight are both powers of two.

133
00:06:14,823 --> 00:06:18,502
Four is two squared,
and eight is two cubed.

134
00:06:18,569 --> 00:06:20,548
Rewrite both sides with base two.

135
00:06:20,568 --> 00:06:24,263
The left exponent becomes two
times the quantity x plus one.

136
00:06:24,283 --> 00:06:27,833
The right becomes three times
the quantity x minus one.

137
00:06:27,853 --> 00:06:30,441
Same base, so the exponents are equal:

138
00:06:30,461 --> 00:06:34,263
two x plus two equals three x minus three.

139
00:06:34,283 --> 00:06:35,513
x equals five.

140
00:06:35,533 --> 00:06:37,140
The answer is C.

141
00:06:37,204 --> 00:06:42,042
Check: four to the sixth and eight
to the fourth are both four thousand

142
00:06:42,062 --> 00:06:43,133
ninety-six.

143
00:06:43,240 --> 00:06:45,362
The wrong choices are slips.

144
00:06:45,382 --> 00:06:47,362
A never distributes.

145
00:06:47,382 --> 00:06:49,898
B distributes on one side only.

146
00:06:49,918 --> 00:06:54,454
D sets x plus one equal to x minus
one without matching the bases.

147
00:06:54,502 --> 00:06:55,446
Question five.

148
00:06:55,466 --> 00:06:59,518
The quantity x minus three,
times the quantity x plus four,

149
00:06:59,538 --> 00:07:03,375
equals five times the
quantity x minus three.

150
00:07:03,395 --> 00:07:06,315
What is the sum of the
solutions to the given equation?

151
00:07:06,335 --> 00:07:07,514
Pause and try it.

152
00:07:11,689 --> 00:07:14,491
It's tempting to divide
both sides by x minus three.

153
00:07:14,511 --> 00:07:18,169
That leaves x plus four
equals five, so x equals one.

154
00:07:18,189 --> 00:07:20,011
But that's only half the answer.

155
00:07:20,033 --> 00:07:23,763
Dividing by x minus three
only works if it isn't zero.

156
00:07:23,783 --> 00:07:26,156
When x is three, it is zero.

157
00:07:26,176 --> 00:07:28,619
Plug in three: both sides become zero.

158
00:07:28,639 --> 00:07:32,354
So three is a solution,
and dividing threw it away.

159
00:07:32,378 --> 00:07:36,786
The safe way: move everything to one
side and factor out x minus three.

160
00:07:36,806 --> 00:07:41,822
You get x minus three, times the
quantity x plus four minus five,

161
00:07:41,842 --> 00:07:45,214
which is x minus three, times x minus one.

162
00:07:45,234 --> 00:07:46,715
The solutions are three and one.

163
00:07:46,735 --> 00:07:48,021
Their sum is four.

164
00:07:48,041 --> 00:07:51,128
If you entered one, you fell for the trap.

165
00:07:51,148 --> 00:07:55,291
Never divide both sides by
an expression with x in it.

166
00:07:55,469 --> 00:07:57,005
Factor it out instead.

167
00:07:57,027 --> 00:07:58,170
Now systems.

168
00:07:58,956 --> 00:08:02,313
When one equation is a line
and the other is a parabola,

169
00:08:02,492 --> 00:08:03,936
use substitution.

170
00:08:03,956 --> 00:08:08,078
Set the two expressions for y
equal, move everything to one side,

171
00:08:08,098 --> 00:08:09,635
and you have a quadratic.

172
00:08:09,665 --> 00:08:12,537
Quick example: y equals
x squared minus one,

173
00:08:12,557 --> 00:08:15,609
and y equals x plus one.

174
00:08:15,629 --> 00:08:21,288
Set them equal, and you get x squared
minus x minus two equals zero.

175
00:08:21,308 --> 00:08:26,379
So x is two or negative one,
and the graphs meet at two,

176
00:08:26,415 --> 00:08:28,735
three and negative one, zero.

177
00:08:28,755 --> 00:08:33,092
Each real solution of that
quadratic is one intersection point.

178
00:08:33,112 --> 00:08:35,627
So the discriminant, b
squared minus four a c,

179
00:08:35,647 --> 00:08:37,127
counts them.

180
00:08:37,147 --> 00:08:38,841
Positive means two points.

181
00:08:38,861 --> 00:08:41,627
Zero means the line just
touches the parabola.

182
00:08:41,647 --> 00:08:43,433
Negative means they never meet.

183
00:08:43,507 --> 00:08:45,416
Circles work the same way.

184
00:08:45,436 --> 00:08:51,166
With x squared plus y squared equals
twenty-five and y equals x plus one,

185
00:08:51,186 --> 00:08:53,987
substitute x plus one for y.

186
00:08:54,007 --> 00:08:59,059
That simplifies to x squared
plus x minus twelve equals zero,

187
00:08:59,079 --> 00:09:03,293
so the points are three,
four and negative four,

188
00:09:03,364 --> 00:09:04,576
negative three.

189
00:09:04,596 --> 00:09:05,846
Question six.

190
00:09:06,132 --> 00:09:11,112
At how many points do the graphs of y
equals x squared minus four x plus nine,

191
00:09:11,132 --> 00:09:16,025
and y equals two x minus one,
intersect in the x y plane?

192
00:09:16,078 --> 00:09:17,257
Pause and try it.

193
00:09:21,432 --> 00:09:22,162
Set them equal.

194
00:09:22,182 --> 00:09:26,377
x squared minus four x plus
nine equals two x minus one.

195
00:09:26,397 --> 00:09:34,670
Move everything to the left: x squared
minus six x plus ten equals zero.

196
00:09:34,690 --> 00:09:35,848
Now the discriminant.

197
00:09:35,868 --> 00:09:37,848
Negative six squared is thirty-six.

198
00:09:37,868 --> 00:09:41,654
Four times one times ten is forty.

199
00:09:41,761 --> 00:09:44,348
Thirty-six minus forty is negative four.

200
00:09:44,368 --> 00:09:48,027
Negative means no real solutions,
so the graphs never meet.

201
00:09:48,047 --> 00:09:49,614
The answer is A.

202
00:09:49,634 --> 00:09:53,828
C is the trap: assuming a line
always cuts a parabola twice.

203
00:09:53,848 --> 00:09:57,026
It can cross twice, touch once, or miss.

204
00:09:57,170 --> 00:10:02,313
B needs a discriminant of zero, and D
is impossible for a line and a parabola.

205
00:10:02,334 --> 00:10:04,442
Question seven, the hardest.

206
00:10:04,905 --> 00:10:07,957
In the system y equals
x squared plus nine,

207
00:10:07,977 --> 00:10:11,136
and y equals m x, m is
a positive constant.

208
00:10:11,156 --> 00:10:14,243
If the system has exactly
one real solution,

209
00:10:14,263 --> 00:10:15,906
what is the value of m?

210
00:10:15,982 --> 00:10:17,196
Pause and try it.

211
00:10:17,232 --> 00:10:18,767
Take a little longer on this one.

212
00:10:22,988 --> 00:10:25,988
Set them equal and move everything left:

213
00:10:26,060 --> 00:10:29,290
x squared minus m x plus nine equals zero.

214
00:10:29,310 --> 00:10:34,076
Exactly one solution means the
line just touches the parabola,

215
00:10:34,096 --> 00:10:36,166
so the discriminant is zero.

216
00:10:36,190 --> 00:10:41,991
Here b is negative m, so m squared
minus four times nine equals zero.

217
00:10:42,011 --> 00:10:45,990
m squared is thirty-six, so
m is six or negative six.

218
00:10:46,010 --> 00:10:48,726
m is positive, so the answer is six.

219
00:10:48,768 --> 00:10:49,534
Two traps.

220
00:10:49,554 --> 00:10:51,891
Thirty-six is m squared, not m.

221
00:10:51,911 --> 00:10:54,713
And negative six also
makes the line touch,

222
00:10:54,733 --> 00:10:57,391
on the left side, which is why
the question says positive.

223
00:10:57,411 --> 00:11:01,462
Check: m equals six gives x minus three,

224
00:11:01,482 --> 00:11:02,426
squared.

225
00:11:02,446 --> 00:11:05,010
One solution, at three, eighteen.

226
00:11:05,030 --> 00:11:10,225
In Desmos, add a slider for m and drag
until the line just touches the parabola.

227
00:11:10,245 --> 00:11:13,082
On the right, that's m
equals six, at three,

228
00:11:13,102 --> 00:11:14,102
eighteen.

229
00:11:14,130 --> 00:11:16,109
Here's the method for test day.

230
00:11:16,129 --> 00:11:19,003
Step one: name the wrapper around x.

231
00:11:19,023 --> 00:11:20,789
Step two: isolate it.

232
00:11:20,809 --> 00:11:25,059
Get the square root or the absolute
value alone before you undo it.

233
00:11:25,144 --> 00:11:27,302
Step three: undo it safely.

234
00:11:27,322 --> 00:11:29,874
Square a root, multiply by denominators,

235
00:11:29,894 --> 00:11:33,552
split absolute value into
two cases, match bases,

236
00:11:33,572 --> 00:11:37,786
substitute in systems, and
factor instead of dividing by x.

237
00:11:37,807 --> 00:11:39,736
Step four: check.

238
00:11:39,807 --> 00:11:44,287
If you squared or cleared fractions,
plug every answer into the original

239
00:11:44,307 --> 00:11:48,625
equation, and cross out any value
that makes a denominator zero.

240
00:11:48,645 --> 00:11:49,983
Or let Desmos do it.

241
00:11:50,003 --> 00:11:53,840
Graph each side as its own equation
and click where they cross.

242
00:11:53,860 --> 00:11:58,875
The x values are the solutions, and fake
solutions never show up as crossings.

243
00:11:58,895 --> 00:12:01,288
For a system, count the crossings.

244
00:12:01,316 --> 00:12:02,153
Recap.

245
00:12:02,173 --> 00:12:05,367
Squaring and clearing fractions
can add fake solutions,

246
00:12:05,387 --> 00:12:06,403
so check.

247
00:12:06,423 --> 00:12:09,581
Dividing by x can lose
real ones, so factor.

248
00:12:09,601 --> 00:12:13,617
Absolute value needs two cases,
exponents need matching bases,

249
00:12:13,637 --> 00:12:17,549
and the discriminant tells you how
many times a line meets a parabola.

250
00:12:17,569 --> 00:12:22,942
Every question in this video was written
for this video in the style of the real

251
00:12:22,962 --> 00:12:23,962
test.

252
00:12:24,176 --> 00:12:28,476
For official practice, the College Board's
